      "Reis's interpersonal process model holds that intimacy grows when one partner discloses and the other responds in a way that feels understanding, validating and caring. Event-contingent diaries confirmed that perceived partner responsiveness (PPR) partially mediates the disclosure–intimacy link, with emotional disclosure mattering more than factual. PPR forecasts affectionate touch, softens how sacrifices are appraised, is amplified by gratitude in an upward spiral, and in a 20-year sample predicted lower all-cause mortality through reduced daily negative-affect reactivity. Listening quality and mindfulness during vulnerability discussions are the trainable inputs. The boundary: PPR is a perception — the responder cannot force it, only make it likely.",
