    description:
      "Dew and Wilcox defined marital generosity as small acts of kindness, regular displays of affection and respect, and a willingness to forgive a spouse's faults, and in a national survey of 1,365 couples it was positively associated with satisfaction and negatively with conflict and perceived divorce likelihood. Kindness is what people rank first in an ideal partner, and in a premarital sample the partner traits that predicted satisfaction for neurotic men were kindness and empathy. The motive matters: sacrifices perceived as made to promote the partner's well-being evoke gratitude; those perceived as self- or relationship-focused do not. The boundary from the myth cluster above applies — in a seriously troubled marriage, kindness alone can mask problems that need action.",
    action: "Each day for a month do one small unrequested kindness for your spouse (coffee made, a task removed, a compliment given in front of others) without mentioning it, and once a week let one minor fault go unremarked.",
